1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,880
 One of the things we've learned from the Snowden documents is that cryptography, broadly applied,

2
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:18,660
 gives the NSA trouble, at least at scale.

3
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:24,980
 So the NSA does a lot of cryptanalysis and they break a lot of systems, but well-designed,

4
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:28,760
 well-implemented cryptography does stymie them.

5
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:35,220
 It's important to understand how it does, because if the NSA wanted to be in my computer,

6
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:42,180
 they'd be in my computer, done, period, no question about it, they would hack into my

7
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:44,780
 computer and they have a lot of tools to do that.

8
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:48,260
 If they are not in my computer, one of two things are true.

9
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:54,900
 One, it's against the law and the NSA is following the law, and two, I'm not high enough on their

10
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:57,460
 priorities list.

11
00:00:57,460 --> 00:01:04,820
 What cryptography does is it forces the attacker, whether the NSA, or the Chinese government,

12
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:10,580
 or cyber criminals, or whoever, to have a priorities list.

13
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:15,700
 And depending on their budget, they'll go down the priorities list and the hope is you're

14
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:20,540
 not there, you are below their budget line.

15
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:30,420
 Without cryptography, an organization like the NSA can bulk collect data on everybody.

16
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:36,820
 With cryptography, they are forced to target, and that's extraordinarily valuable, because

17
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:44,380
 it means the FBI will go after the criminals, and the NSA will go after the agents of a

18
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:52,300
 foreign power, the Chinese government will go after the US government officials that

19
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:55,540
 rise to whatever level they want to spy on.

20
00:01:55,540 --> 00:02:01,420
 The cyber criminals will just go after a few of us, and the rest of us are protected.

21
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:05,300
 That makes cryptography a very important tool.

22
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:12,820
 Now, cryptography doesn't actually provide any security, because cryptography is mathematics.

23
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:19,380
 When we say we trust the cryptography, what we're saying is we trust the mathematics,

24
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:21,800
 and I think there's a lot of reason to say that.

25
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,760
 I trust the mathematics.

26
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:29,740
 Everything I know about cryptography tells me the mathematics is good.

27
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:33,460
 Certainly there will be cryptographic advances, certainly some things will be broken in the

28
00:02:33,460 --> 00:02:37,180
 future, but by and large, the math works.

29
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:39,920
 But math has no agency.

30
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:41,820
 Math can't do anything.

31
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:43,380
 It's equations on a piece of paper.

32
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:51,380
 In order for math to do something, someone has to take that math and write code, and

33
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:56,860
 embed that code in a program, and embed that program in some bigger system, and put that

34
00:02:56,860 --> 00:03:02,460
 bigger system on a computer with an operating system, on a network, with a user, and all

35
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:06,380
 of those things add in security.

36
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:11,300
 When the NSA breaks cryptography, by and large, they don't break the mathematics.

37
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:13,020
 They break something else.

38
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:14,180
 They break the implementation.

39
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:15,700
 They break the software.

40
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:17,260
 They break the network.

41
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:19,900
 They break the hardware the software is running on.

42
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:25,300
 They do something somewhere else, and again and again we learn this lesson.

43
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:31,260
 The math works, but putting stuff around it is much harder.

44
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:37,880
 Now there's an important corollary here, that complexity is the worst enemy of security.

45
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:40,500
 What these things do is they add complexity.

46
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:46,060
 The more complex you make your system, the less secure it's going to be because the more

47
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:49,020
 vulnerabilities you'll have.

48
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:55,300
 The more mistakes you'll make somewhere in that system, and we learn again and again

49
00:03:55,300 --> 00:04:02,380
 when we see analyses of voting systems, embedded systems, your cell phone, messaging systems,

50
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:06,140
 email systems, that it's always something around the crypto.

51
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:12,620
 Something that the designers, the implementers, the coders, the users, got wrong.

52
00:04:12,620 --> 00:04:17,540
 The simpler we can make systems, the more secure they are.

53
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:25,500
 What NIST is doing is they're trying to build standards around as much as possible.

54
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:28,380
 They have a standard for a crypto algorithm.

55
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:31,140
 AES is the standard crypto algorithm.

56
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:38,160
 It was a public process where multiple groups submitted algorithms, and the community as

57
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:41,580
 a whole picked a winner.

58
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:45,700
 It wasn't dictated on high, there weren't secret criteria.

59
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:51,220
 The AES algorithm was the one that most of us thought should be AES.

60
00:04:51,220 --> 00:04:54,580
 Actually, there were several we thought were good candidates.

61
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:59,220
 They picked one, but there's a lot of trust in the process because it was a very public,

62
00:04:59,220 --> 00:05:01,500
 open, international process.

63
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:06,940
 SHA-3, the new secure hash standard, the same sort of process.

64
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:09,900
 It's really fun as a cryptographer to be involved in this process.

65
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:12,500
 I think of it as a great crypto demolition derby.

66
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:14,900
 We all put our algorithms in the ring, beat each other up.

67
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:16,580
 The last one left standing wins.

68
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:18,500
 It was kind of like that.

69
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:24,620
 We would all publish papers analyzing each other, and one of the ones left standing won.

70
00:05:24,620 --> 00:05:28,900
 That's just a small part of what NIST does.

71
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:31,140
 They have standards for random number generation.

72
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:37,420
 They have standards for key agreement, for different protocols, trying to standardize

73
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:43,200
 these components so the implementers make fewer mistakes.

74
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:48,660
 But still, there's a lot that you can't standardize, and those bigger pieces are where you're going

75
00:05:48,660 --> 00:05:50,260
 to still find most of your vulnerabilities.

76
00:05:50,260 --> 00:05:55,140
 I believe that's where the NSA finds most of its vulnerabilities, that it's out there.

77
00:05:55,140 --> 00:06:00,340
 Recently, we learned about vulnerabilities in the key agreement protocols that are used

78
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:03,780
 to secure a lot of the VPNs and internet connections.

79
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:09,940
 If you look at where that vulnerability was, it's because of a shortcut that was made and

80
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:12,700
 copy that allowed for a massive pre-computation.

81
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:14,340
 The math worked great.

82
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:20,020
 If you want to make a standard worse, you make it super complex, and you're just building

83
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:22,380
 in vectors at that point.

84
00:06:22,380 --> 00:06:27,700
 This is why the normal IETF process for internet standards doesn't really work for security,

85
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:31,780
 because those standards are compromises.

86
00:06:31,780 --> 00:06:34,360
 Let's put in all the options to make everyone happy.

87
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:39,720
 Let's put in as much flexibility as necessary to make the system as comprehensive as possible.

88
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:42,260
 That is anti-secure security.

89
00:06:42,260 --> 00:06:47,340
 Security needs as few options as possible, as simple as possible.

90
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:48,340
 You don't want compromise.

91
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:53,900
 You want one group to win, because that group has a self-contained vision.

92
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:56,860
 When you have a piece of this, and a piece of that, and a piece of that, there's going

93
00:06:56,860 --> 00:07:00,900
 to be some interaction you didn't notice, and that interaction will be the interaction

94
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:03,380
 that breaks your system.

95
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:06,140
 You didn't win AES, right?

96
00:07:06,140 --> 00:07:07,140
 You were in it.

97
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:09,700
 You were in the demolition derby with your helmet on.

98
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:13,180
 Tell me a little bit about what it's like to be in the demolition derby toward the end,

99
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:16,700
 and what it's like to sort of not win the demolition derby.

100
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:18,780
 So AES was an interesting process.

101
00:07:18,780 --> 00:07:26,540
 It started out with 64 algorithms, which 56 met the submission criteria, then NIST whittled

102
00:07:26,540 --> 00:07:32,540
 it down to, I think it was 15 or 16, and then in the next round whittled it down to 5, and

103
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:33,540
 then chose 1.

104
00:07:33,540 --> 00:07:39,820
 So it's a constant winnowing process, and Twofish, which was my submission, made it

105
00:07:39,820 --> 00:07:43,980
 all the way into the top 5.

106
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:45,460
 Those top 5 were all good algorithms.

107
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:50,220
 I mean, there was no bad algorithms there, and the arguments were more about security

108
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:59,020
 margin and implementability in hardware versus embedded systems versus constrained systems,

109
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:01,020
 8-bit, 32-bit.

110
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:07,740
 So we were making distinctions about how we thought it would be used, and to me, it came

111
00:08:07,740 --> 00:08:13,060
 down to, I think, 3 algorithms, and I thought these were all good choices.

112
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:17,540
 Twofish was one, Ringdahl, the eventual Winder, was one, and actually, at this point, I forget

113
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:19,460
 what the third is.

114
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:25,860
 And what I said on my blog at the time is, you know, any of these three are good.

115
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:29,300
 And sure, it would have been great to have been the winner, but there really is something.

116
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:34,740
 There's a lot of value in NIST picking a non-US algorithm.

117
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:40,940
 By picking an algorithm from Belgium, it said to the world that NIST is picking what they

118
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:45,620
 thought was the best, and not trying to pick American.

119
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:50,860
 So that was an important consideration I hadn't thought of at the time.

120
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:52,940
 So I can't fault in this process at all.

121
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:54,340
 It's been great to win.

122
00:08:54,340 --> 00:09:00,240
 It actually was really fun to participate, and I would do it again, and I participated

123
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:07,300
 in the SHA-3 competition, which again was picked by someone else won.

124
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:10,180
 My entry was called "Scane."

125
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:17,460
 And you know, these are lots of fun for cryptographers and also for students, because they give students

126
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:18,460
 a whole bunch of targets.

127
00:09:18,460 --> 00:09:22,820
 One of the hard things if you're a crypto student is you have to break stuff.

128
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:25,700
 The only way you learn how to make things is by breaking things.

129
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:28,180
 It's back to that security mindset.

130
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:31,040
 Anybody can create a security system that he can't break.

131
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:34,500
 So if you show up with a security system and say, "I can't break this," my first question

132
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:36,420
 is, "Well, who are you?

133
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:41,740
 Why should I trust your attestation that you can't break it as something that's meaningful?

134
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:43,980
 What else have you broken?"

135
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:50,540
 And these competitions give a whole bunch of targets so students can start breaking things

136
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:57,780
 that haven't been broken before, get papers out of it, get publications, get cred in the

137
00:09:57,780 --> 00:10:04,260
 field as someone who can break stuff and therefore as someone who can design stuff.

138
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:06,120
 It's a source of new problems.

139
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:07,740
 It's a source of new targets.

140
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:10,700
 But this is what I said to start.

141
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:13,740
 Security is inherently adversarial.

142
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:18,060
 And that adversarial nature makes it different.

143
00:10:18,060 --> 00:10:23,620
 Unlike any other field in computer science, you go to a security conference, a crypto

144
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:29,180
 conference, and there are going to be papers of people who break each other's stuff.

145
00:10:29,180 --> 00:10:32,020
 And you have to get a thick skin.

146
00:10:32,020 --> 00:10:35,900
 You have to understand that we are all learning.

147
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:41,140
 If I produce a protocol and you break it, sure, I'm unhappy, but I've learned something.

148
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:43,500
 So have you and so has everyone else.

149
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:51,340
 And that knowledge is more important than my particular creation surviving.

150
00:10:51,340 --> 00:10:54,660
 And you have to understand that and accept that, and that has to excite you.

